{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"DOI:\"10.1016/j.peptides.2012.03.029\"","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"22497805","source":"MED","pmid":"22497805","doi":"10.1016/j.peptides.2012.03.029","title":"The hymenochirins: a family of host-defense peptides from the Congo dwarf clawed frog Hymenochirus boettgeri (Pipidae).","authorString":"Mechkarska M, Prajeep M, Coquet L, Leprince J, Jouenne T, Vaudry H, King JD, Conlon JM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mechkarska M","lastName":"Mechkarska","firstName":"Milena","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8882-0975"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, United Arab Emirates University, 17666 Al-Ain, United Arab Emirates."}]}},{"fullName":"Prajeep M","lastName":"Prajeep","firstName":"Manju","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Coquet L","lastName":"Coquet","firstName":"Laurent","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7778-7321"}},{"fullName":"Leprince J","lastName":"Leprince","firstName":"Jérôme","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7814-9927"}},{"fullName":"Jouenne T","lastName":"Jouenne","firstName":"Thierry","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0994-9944"}},{"fullName":"Vaudry H","lastName":"Vaudry","firstName":"Hubert","initials":"H"},{"fullName":"King JD","lastName":"King","firstName":"Jay D","initials":"JD"},{"fullName":"Conlon JM","lastName":"Conlon","firstName":"J Michael","initials":"JM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4343-1551"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7778-7321"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8882-0975"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4343-1551"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7814-9927"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0994-9944"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"35","journalIssueId":1933425,"dateOfPublication":"2012 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2012,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Peptides","title":"Peptides","essn":"1873-5169","issn":"0079-0753","isoabbreviation":"Peptides","nlmid":"8008690"},"printPublicationDate":"2012-06-01"},"pubYear":"2012","pageInfo":"269-275","abstractText":"Skin secretions of frogs from the subfamily Xenopodinae (Xenopus+Silurana) within the family Pipidae are a rich source of antimicrobial peptides with therapeutic potential but species from the sister taxon Hymenochirus in the subfamily Pipinae (Hymenochirus+Pseudhymenochirus+Pipa) have not been investigated. Peptidomic analysis of norepinephrine-stimulated skin secretions from two distinct populations of the Congo dwarf clawed frog Hymenochirus boettgeri (Tornier, 1896) has led to identification of five structurally related peptides with broad-spectrum antimicrobial activity. Hymenochirin-1B (IKLSPETKDNLKKVLKGAIKGAIAVAKMV.NH(2)) is C-terminally α-amidated whereas hymenochirins-2B-5B have the general structure XKIPX(2)VKDTLKKVAKGX(2)SX(2)AGAX(3).COOH. Hymenochirin-3B (IKIPAVVKDTLKKVAKGVLSAVAGALTQ) was the most abundant peptide in the secretions. The hymenochirins show very low structural similarity with the antimicrobial peptides isolated from skin secretions of Silurana tropicalis and Xenopus laevis consistent with the proposed ancient divergence of the Pipinae and Xenopodinae. Synthetic replicates of hymenochirin-1B-4B inhibit the growth of Escherichia coli, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Klebsiella pneumoniae, and Staphylococcus aureus (MIC in the range 10-40 μM) and Candida albicans (MIC=80 μM). The peptides display relatively weak hemolytic activity against human erythrocytes (LC(50) in the range 160 to >300 μM).","affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, United Arab Emirates University, 17666 Al-Ain, United Arab Emirates.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"University Research Grant","grantId":"G00000900","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Faculty Support Grant","grantId":"NP/12/03","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Erythrocytes","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Skin","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pipidae","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Escherichia coli","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Klebsiella pneumoniae","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Staphylococcus aureus","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Candida albicans","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Antimicrobial Cationic Peptides","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IP","qualifierName":"isolation & purification"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbrev